 2. 👀 Scoop: AOC&#x27;s staff shake-up 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios. Photo: Eva Marie Uzcategui/Bloomberg via Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Rep. Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez (D-N.Y.) is shaking up her team with several departures and new aides, sources tell Axios' Alex Thompson and Holly Otterbein. 

 Why it matters: The personnel changes come months before the progressive leader will decide whether to launch a presidential campaign. She's also weighing a run for Senate Minority Leader Chuck Schumer's seat, which is up in 2028. ⚡ Driving the news: Oliver Hidalgo-Wohlleben, AOC's campaign manager and senior adviser for the past three years, is departing in September, according to two people familiar with the matter.

 Fiorella Bini, a longtime aide to AOC, took over as interim campaign manager this month. Aya Saed, Ocasio-Cortez's former legislative director, returned to her operation this spring as a deputy chief of staff in her House office and an adviser to her campaign. Tanushri Shankar-Ross, who left AOC's team in 2023, has come back as a senior adviser, Puck first reported. Tyler Evans, AOC's creative director who had been with her since 2021 — sometimes in a freelance role — also left this summer, according to his LinkedIn page. The intrigue: The moves coincide with the expansion of the portfolio of AOC's chief of staff Mike Casca, people familiar with her operation said.

 Casca was senior communications adviser for Sen. Bernie Sanders' (I-Vt.) 2020 presidential campaign, and then deputy chief of staff in Sanders' Senate office before joining Ocasio-Cortez's team in late 2023. Read on . 

 
 
