 6. 🇳🇵 Struck without warning 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 A riverside after flash floods at Trishuli Bazaar in Nepal's Nuwakot district this week. Photo: Prabin Ranabhat/AFP via Getty Images Nepal's catastrophic flood is exposing gaps in the warning systems designed to watch out for extreme rain and rivers — not for sudden collapses high in the mountains during good weather, Axios' Herb Scribner reports. 

 Wednesday's flash floods didn't come with any of the typical red flags — no thunderstorms, no rainfall, no rise in the river, no earthquakes. The big picture: Such glacier-driven floods under clear skies — sometimes called "blue-sky floods" — can happen in the U.S. too, especially in Alaska and the Pacific Northwest, where sudden releases of glacial water can send floods and debris racing down mountain valleys.

 The latest: Rescue operations entered the fourth day today.

 Nearly 3,000 people are still missing and almost 700 have died, according to Nepali officials' updated count . Read on . 

 
 
